 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Third-voltage trip point",
   "expr": "VEXT_TH = 1.22V × (R1 + R2) / R2",
   "where": {
    "VEXT_TH": "voltage of the watched rail at which reset asserts",
    "1.22V": "typical RSTIN threshold, VRSTIN",
    "R1": "resistor from the rail to RSTIN",
    "R2": "resistor from RSTIN to GND"
   },
   "note": "MAX6355/MAX6356/MAX6357.",
   "page": 7
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Supplies",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The chip runs from whichever of VCC1 and VCC2 is higher, and watches both. The reset outputs keep a valid state as long as one of the two supplies is above 1 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thresholds",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "A two-letter suffix picks the factory-set pair of thresholds: the first letter sets VTH1, the second VTH2. When either supply drops under its threshold, every reset output of the part asserts. The threshold hysteresis is VTH/500."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reset outputs",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The MAX6351 has two push-pull outputs, RST1 driven from VCC1 and RST2 from VCC2. The MAX6353, MAX6356 and MAX6359 have one push-pull output driven from VCC1; the MAX6354, MAX6357 and MAX6360 one push-pull output driven from VCC2; the MAX6352, MAX6355 and MAX6358 one open-drain output. All are active low. After every supply is back above its threshold the output stays asserted for the reset timeout period, 100 ms to 280 ms."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply transients",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Short negative dips on VCC1 and VCC2 are ignored. A dip resets the part only when it lasts longer than the maximum transient duration for its overdrive below the threshold."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Manual reset",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Every part has a debounced, active-low MR input with an internal pullup (32 kΩ to 100 kΩ). Pulses shorter than about 100 ns are rejected; 1 µs low is enough to reset."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Third voltage monitor (MAX6355/MAX6356/MAX6357)",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "RSTIN compares an outside voltage against a 1.22 V reference; a resistor divider sets the trip point of a rail above 1.22 V. The comparator is powered from VCC1, so RSTIN must not go above VCC1."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Watchdog (MAX6358/MAX6359/MAX6360)",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The watchdog has two timeouts. After any reset (power-up, manual reset or a watchdog timeout) a long start-up timeout runs (tWD, 25.6 s to 72.0 s), giving the system time to boot. The first edge on WDI, or the end of the start-up timeout, switches it to the normal timeout (1.6 s to 4.5 s). In normal mode a missing WDI edge within the timeout produces a reset pulse of the reset timeout period. Any rising or falling edge on WDI restarts the timer. An open WDI disables the watchdog."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reset held to 0 V",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "To keep reset valid while both supplies fall to 0 V, a 100 kΩ resistor from a push-pull reset output to ground is used. The open-drain parts cannot use this."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Processors with bidirectional reset pins",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "A push-pull reset output contends with a processor reset pin that also drives. A 4.7 kΩ resistor between the two prevents it; a buffer on the processor side feeds any other part that needs reset."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Process",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "BiCMOS."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Ordering",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The two underscores in each part number take the threshold suffix. Parts ship on tape and reel only, in a minimum order increment of 2500 for the SOT package. Replacing \"-T\" with \"+T\" orders the lead(Pb)-free version. Sample stock is held for the standard suffixes LS, TZ, TW, SY, SV and RV."
  }
 ]
}
